  {
   "xml_index": 19,
   "xml_name": "PS5_019_Recursive List Product",
   "display_name": "Recursive List Product",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Write a recursive function <code>list_product(lst, index)</code> that takes a list of integers <code>lst</code> and a position <code>index</code> (where <code>0 &lt;= index &lt;= len(lst)</code>), and <b>returns</b> the product of the elements from position <code>index</code> up to the end. If there are no elements left (<code>index</code> is equal to <code>len(lst)</code>), the product is 1.</p>\n<p>Your function <b>must be recursive</b> (it must call itself), and must <b>not</b> use any loop.</p>\n<p><b>Python facts you may use:</b> <code>len(lst)</code> gives the number of elements in a list, and <code>lst[i]</code> gives the element at position <code>i</code> (positions start from 0, so the last position is <code>len(lst) - 1</code>).</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b> <code>list_product([2, 3, 4], 0)</code> returns <code>24</code>. <code>list_product([2, 3, 4], 2)</code> returns <code>4</code>. <code>list_product([2, 3, 4], 3)</code> returns <code>1</code>. <code>list_product([5, -2, 0, 7], 0)</code> returns <code>0</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(list_product([2, 3, 4], 0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "24",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(list_product([2, 3, 4], 2))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "4",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(list_product([2, 3, 4], 3))",
